```java
final class Money {
    private final long cents;
    private final String currency;
    Money(long cents, String currency) { this.cents = cents; this.currency = currency; }
    Money plus(Money other) { /* returns a new Money, never mutates */ return this; }
}
```

Without `final`, a subclass could override `plus()` to mutate `this` instead of returning a new instance, silently breaking every caller that relies on `Money` being immutable — including code that safely shares `Money` instances across threads based on that exact guarantee. `final` makes that impossible to introduce by accident.

```java
sealed interface PaymentResult permits Approved, Declined, Pending {}
record Approved(String txnId) implements PaymentResult {}
record Declined(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
record Pending(String checkUrl) implements PaymentResult {}

String describe(PaymentResult r) {
    return switch (r) {
        case Approved a -> "OK: " + a.txnId();
        case Declined d -> "Failed: " + d.reason();
        case Pending p -> "Waiting: " + p.checkUrl();
        // no default needed — the compiler knows these are the only three
    };
}
```

Because `permits` enumerates every subtype, the compiler can verify the `switch` covers all of them — no `default` branch, and no risk of a silently-unhandled new case slipping in later.
